<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tenskwatawa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1775">1775</a>–<a href="/years/1836">1836</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Shawnee Prophet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tenskwatawa, known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shawnee">Shawnee</a> Prophet, was a Shawnee religious leader and the brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tecumseh">Tecumseh</a>. After a vision in 1805 he preached the rejection of alcohol and American goods and a return to Native ways, inspiring a movement across many nations. His standing fell after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-tippecanoe">Battle of Tippecanoe</a> in 1811; he later lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kansas">Kansas</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tenskwatawa.jpg" alt="Tenskwatawa" width="320" height="389" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Catlin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ten-sq%C3%BAat-a-way.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born Lalawethika, He Makes a Loud Noise, in early 1775, he was one of triplets born to Puckeshinwa and Methoataske in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shawnee">Shawnee</a> village on the Mad River in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>. His father, a leader of the Kispokotha division, had been killed at the Battle of Point Pleasant, and the children were raised by their older sister Tecumpeas. Overshadowed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tecumseh">Tecumseh</a>, he failed as a hunter and warrior, lost the sight of his right eye to his own arrow and drank heavily. He was at the Battle of Fallen Timbers in 1794 and later trained as a medicine man under the healer Penagashea.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-greenville">Treaty of Greenville</a> of 1795, Governor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-henry-harrison">William Henry Harrison</a> pressed pro-accommodation chiefs to cede more land, often for far less than its market price, while settlers moved in and sold alcohol. In May 1805, reviving as his body was being prepared for burial, Lalawethika told of a vision of two worlds and took the name Tenskwatawa, Open Door. He forbade marriage with whites and Euro-American food, clothing and goods, and accused opponents, including Christian converts, of witchcraft. When Harrison challenged him to perform a miracle, he correctly predicted the eclipse of 16 June 1806.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophetstown">Prophetstown</a>, founded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tecumseh">Tecumseh</a> in 1808 where the Wabash and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tippecanoe-river">Tippecanoe Rivers</a> meet, drew some 3,000 people from an estimated 14 tribal groups, most of them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shawnee">Shawnee</a>, Delaware and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/potawatomi">Potawatomi</a>. The brothers denounced the Treaty of Fort Wayne of 1809. With Tecumseh away in the South, Tenskwatawa's 600 to 700 men attacked Harrison's camp of about 1,000 before dawn on 7 November 1811, assured that his spells would protect them; he stayed out of range. After the defeat some wanted to kill him, and he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a>, where he fled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-thames">Battle of the Thames</a> in October 1813.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In 1824, at the request of Lewis Cass, governor of Michigan Territory, he returned to help with the removal of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shawnee">Shawnee</a> west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mississippi-river">Mississippi</a>, reaching their reservation in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kansas">Kansas</a> on 14 May 1828 and founding another <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prophetstown">Prophetstown</a> there. George Catlin painted his portrait. He died in his cabin in November 1836; the White Feather Spring marker of 1978 shows the approximate place of his grave. Historians have disagreed over whether he or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tecumseh">Tecumseh</a> led the movement, but he gave the resistance before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-1812">War of 1812</a> its spiritual foundation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tenskwatawa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Governor William Henry Harrison of the Indiana Territory marched about 1,000 men against Prophetstown, headquarters of the confederacy of Tecumseh and his brother <b>Tenskwatawa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-7">7 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Tenskwatawa</b>, known as the Prophet, preached rejection of American goods and ways and a return to Native traditions.</blockquote><a href="/state/tecumsehs-confederacy">Tecumseh's Confederacy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohio">Ohio</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kansas">Kansas</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.5,-82.5&amp;year=1814" aria-label="Ohio on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Tenskwatawa"><image href="/maps/globes/g270n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5486 4091h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5486 4091h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.5,-82.5&amp;year=1814">What was here in 1814? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
